## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical rate-limiting design for multi-tenant LLM gateways, focusing on fairness, latency guarantees, and graceful degradation under burst conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant quotas with burst handling capabilities
- SLA enforcement and backpressure mechanisms
- Comprehensive observability and rollback safety measures

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    return this.tokens >= 1;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This angle tests practical cost-aware routing for a real multi-tenant gateway, a common beginner task with clear trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant budgets and quotas
- Simple model tier mapping and latency targets
- Atomic budget deduction and canary behavior
- Caching for repeated prompts
- Observability and test coverage

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectModel(tenant, latencyTargetMs){
  const models = {
    fast: {cost:0.02, latency:80},
    balanced: {cost:0.01, latency:200},
    accurate: {cost:0.04, latency:400}
  };
  const tiers = ['fast','balanced','accurate'].filter(t => models[t].latency <= latencyTargetMs && tenant.budget >= models[t].cost);
  return tiers.length? tiers[0] : 'fast';
}
```

